<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps on the Mac — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same comparison as last time, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos hands its decodes to Apple, while LightCraft does every decode in its own Rust. That changes which app is faster, and where."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time> &middot; updated <time datetime="2026-10-09">October 9</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same comparison as <a
          href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">last
          time</a>, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos hands its decodes
          to Apple, while
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          does every decode in its own Rust. That changes which app is faster,
          and where.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 10 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Updated October 9. LightPhotos now develops the photos on either
          side ahead of time, as LightCraft does, and decodes full-size RAW
          files with CoreImage's <code>CIRAWFilter</code>. In a full-screen
          window on a 4K display, the next photo now finishes in
          0.55&nbsp;s. The October 7 build took 1.10&nbsp;s and LightCraft
          0.60&nbsp;s, and both new times are at the floor of what my script
          can measure. LightPhotos still fills its grid faster, 0.85&nbsp;s
          to 1.37&nbsp;s, and uses a little less memory, 1.2&nbsp;GB to
          1.4&nbsp;GB. A full-size RAW decode went from 590&nbsp;ms to
          185&nbsp;ms, which you get when you zoom in or export.
        </p>
      </aside>

