      <p>
        The runs agreed to within a few percent, with three exceptions. The
        first photo finished in 1.0 to 1.5&nbsp;s in both LightPhotos
        builds. One LightCraft grid took 2.3&nbsp;s. LightCraft's memory
        went from 1.3 to 1.7&nbsp;GB. At this window size, both apps
        showed all 20 thumbnails.
      </p>

      <h2>What changed in LightPhotos</h2>
      <p>
        In the first version of this post, LightPhotos reacted as fast as
        LightCraft but finished the next photo about 0.7&nbsp;s later. It
        showed the camera's embedded JPEG at once, and only asked ImageIO
        to develop the RAW data when you arrived. Two changes since then
        close that gap.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It prepares the photos on either side.</strong> When the
        photo on screen is done, LightPhotos develops its two neighbours in
        the background, at the window's size, and keeps them. The arrow key
        then only has to put an image on screen that is already there. The
        next photo finished in 0.55&nbsp;s, half the 1.10&nbsp;s of the
        October 7 build, and the screen changed only once, with no soft
        JPEG first. LightCraft took 0.60&nbsp;s. Both are at the floor of
        the measurement, so I would call them equal.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It decodes full-size RAW files with
        <code>CIRAWFilter</code>.</strong> This is CoreImage's RAW API, one
        level below ImageIO. It runs the same Apple RAW engine, so the
        pixels agree to within 8-bit rounding, but it renders on the GPU
        and demosaics at the size you ask for. On the same twenty files,
        one at a time, a full-size 24-megapixel decode took a median of
        185&nbsp;ms, against 590&nbsp;ms through ImageIO. That does not show
        in the table. At this window size, ImageIO's develop at 4096&nbsp;px
        already has every pixel that the screen shows, so the app never asks
        for the full size. You get the faster decode when you zoom in to
        100% and when you export.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both changes cost something. The first photo now finishes later,
        1.46&nbsp;s against 1.12&nbsp;s. At this size the embedded JPEG, 1616&nbsp;px wide, is smaller
        than the window, so LightPhotos skips it, shows the grid thumbnail
        and develops the RAW data straight away. It also develops both
        neighbours at the same time. Memory went from 1.0 to 1.2&nbsp;GB,
        because two developed neighbours stay in memory, about 45&nbsp;MB each at
        4096&nbsp;px. And LightPhotos now makes the same trade as LightCraft:
        it spends CPU time and battery on develops that you might never
        look at.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft develops at up to 2560&nbsp;px, and it also decodes and
        develops both neighbours in the background. My script waits a few
        seconds on each photo, which gives both apps time to get ahead.
        That is about the pace of a person who looks at each photo. If you
        hold the arrow key down, you could move faster than either app can
        prepare. I did not measure that.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the grid and the memory go the other way</h2>
      <p>
        For a grid thumbnail, LightPhotos asks ImageIO for an image no bigger
        than the thumbnail, and ImageIO answers from the embedded JPEG in 12 to 22&nbsp;ms per
        file. So twenty RAW files fill the grid in under a second.
        LightCraft also shows the embedded JPEGs first, but then replaces
        them with thumbnails that it develops from the RAW data, and its grid
        is not finished until those arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        For memory, LightCraft keeps the decoded RAW data for the current
        photo and both neighbours as 32-bit floating-point RGB, at 12 bytes
        a pixel. That is about 52&nbsp;MB per photo at 2560&nbsp;px. This
        data lets its sliders use the full range of the sensor: highlights
        above white, and exposure changes without banding. LightPhotos gets
        finished 8-bit images from ImageIO, at 4 bytes a pixel, but at
        4096&nbsp;px, so it now keeps about the same amount of image data
        for the same three photos. On screen, the two look the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app size is ImageIO again. LightCraft ships its own decoders for
        RAW, JPEG, JPEG XL and more, plus its own colour management, and its release build skips link-time
        optimisation and keeps some debug information. The macOS decoders
        cost LightPhotos nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What ImageIO costs</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO is not free in every sense. The photos look the way Apple
        develops them, which is not the same as LightPhotos on Linux, on
        Windows or in the browser. LightPhotos supports only the cameras
        that Apple supports, when Apple supports them. When ImageIO decodes
        a file incorrectly, I cannot fix it. <code>CIRAWFilter</code> runs
        the same RAW engine, so it changes none of this.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft gets the opposite trade. One decoder and one develop
        everywhere means the same photo looks the same on every platform,
        and LightCraft can fix any bug in its decoder. The cost is a bigger
        app and the formats it has not written yet. HEIC needs an HEVC decoder, and no
        pure-Rust one with a permissive license exists yet. CR3 files show
        only their embedded preview for now.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        On the Mac, LightPhotos relies on the operating system, and
        LightCraft relies on itself. LightPhotos is smaller, starts faster,
        fills its grid faster and uses a little less memory, because Apple
        already did that work, and because a much smaller feature set fits
        in a smaller app. Now that it prepares the photos on either side,
        stepping through a shoot is as quick as in LightCraft. LightCraft
        looks the same everywhere and does far more when you start to edit.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Two Rust photo apps in the browser</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/linux-raw-photo-editor.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">A fast RAW photo editor for Linux</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    // Steps through a row of nine photos. LightPhotos now and LightCraft
    // develop both neighbours of the photo on screen; the October 7 build of
    // LightPhotos developed only the photo on screen.
    const fig = document.getElementById("step-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const N = 9;
      const START = 1;
      // Seconds for one step, from the table: [reacts, finished].
      const TIMES = { lp: [0.55, 0.55], old: [0.54, 1.1], lc: [0.6, 0.6] } as const;
      const PX_PER_S = 516 / 1.2;
      const NEAR = { lp: "ready", old: "JPEG", lc: "ready" } as const;
      const PREPARES = { lp: true, old: false, lc: true } as const;
      type App = keyof typeof TIMES;
      const APPS = Object.keys(TIMES) as App[];

      const q = (sel: string) => fig.querySelector(sel) as SVGElement;
      const el = (name: string) => q(`[data-el="${name}"]`);
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout") as HTMLElement;
      const nextBtn = fig.querySelector('[data-act="next"]') as HTMLButtonElement;
      const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");

      let cur: number, dropped: number[], frame = 0;
      const dev = { lp: 0, old: 0, lc: 0 };
      const unused = { lp: 0, old: 0, lc: 0 };

      const neighbours = (i: number) => [i - 1, i + 1].filter((n) => n >= 0 && n < N);
      const plural = (n: number, word: string) => `${n} ${word}${n === 1 ? "" : "s"}`;

      function cell(app: App, i: number) {
        let cls = "lp-fig-panel", text = "", textCls = "lp-fig-s";
        if (i === cur) { cls = "lp-fig-box-accent"; text = "now"; textCls = "lp-fig-t"; }
        else if (neighbours(cur).includes(i)) { cls = "lp-fig-box"; text = NEAR[app]; }
        else if (PREPARES[app] && dropped.includes(i)) { cls = "lp-fig-bad-stroke"; text = "unused"; }
        q(`[data-cell="${app}${i}"]`).setAttribute("class", cls);
        const label = q(`[data-label="${app}${i}"]`);
        label.setAttribute("class", textCls);
        label.textContent = text;
      }

      function bars(app: App, t: number | null) {
        const [react, finish] = TIMES[app];
        const r = t === null ? 0 : Math.min(t, react);
        const f = t === null ? 0 : Math.max(0, Math.min(t, finish) - react);
        el(`${app}-react`).setAttribute("width", String(r * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-finish`).setAttribute("x", String(react * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-finish`).setAttribute("width", String(f * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-time`).textContent =
          t === null ? "not timed" : t >= finish ? `${react.toFixed(2)} / ${finish.toFixed(2)} s` : `${Math.min(t, finish).toFixed(2)} s`;
      }

      function render() {
        for (const app of APPS) {
          for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) cell(app, i);
          el(`${app}-dev`).textContent = plural(dev[app], "develop");
          el(`${app}-unused`).textContent = `${unused[app]} unused`;
        }
        nextBtn.disabled = cur === N - 1;
      }

      const counts = () =>
        `LightPhotos now: ${plural(dev.lp, "develop")}, ${unused.lp} unused. LightCraft: ${plural(dev.lc, "develop")}, ${unused.lc} unused. October 7 build: ${plural(dev.old, "develop")}, 0 unused.`;

      function moveTo(target: number) {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        const ready = neighbours(cur);
        const wasReady = ready.includes(target);
        dropped = ready.filter((n) => n !== target);
        const fresh = (wasReady ? 0 : 1) + neighbours(target).filter((n) => n !== cur).length;
        for (const app of APPS) {
          if (PREPARES[app]) { unused[app] += dropped.length; dev[app] += fresh; }
          else dev[app] += 1;
        }
        cur = target;
        render();
      }

      function step() {
        if (cur >= N - 1) return;
        moveTo(cur + 1);
        const done = () => {
          for (const app of APPS) bars(app, 9);
          readout.textContent = `Photo ${cur + 1}: LightPhotos is finished at 0.55 s and LightCraft at 0.60 s. The October 7 build reacted at 0.54 s and finished at 1.10 s. ${counts()}`;
        };
        if (reduce.matches) return done();
        const start = performance.now();
        const tick = (now: number) => {
          const t = Math.max(0, now - start) / 1000;
          for (const app of APPS) bars(app, t);
          if (t < TIMES.old[1]) frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
          else done();
        };
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }

      function jump() {
        moveTo((cur + 4) % N);
        for (const app of APPS) bars(app, null);
        readout.textContent = `Jump to photo ${cur + 1}: no app had developed it. I did not time this case. ${counts()}`;
      }

      function reset() {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        cur = START; dropped = [];
        dev.lp = 3; dev.lc = 3; dev.old = 1;
        unused.lp = 0; unused.lc = 0; unused.old = 0;
        render();
        for (const app of APPS) bars(app, 9);
        readout.textContent = "Photo 2 of 9. Press Next photo to step, or Jump in the grid to skip ahead.";
      }

      reset();
      nextBtn.addEventListener("click", step);
      fig.querySelector('[data-act="jump"]')!.addEventListener("click", jump);
      fig.querySelector('[data-act="reset"]')!.addEventListener("click", reset);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
